# Security
- No credentials, keys, tokens or connection strings with passwords in source, tests, docs or
  commits. Read them from environment or config providers.
- Validate and normalise all untrusted input (HTTP, files, queues, env) at the trust boundary.
- SQL is always parameterised. No string-built SQL, including in stored procedures (`sp_executesql` with params).
- Every new endpoint or handler states its authorization. Missing auth is a defect, not a default.
- Return minimal error detail to clients. Keep stack traces and PII server-side.
- Never disable TLS checks, CSRF/CORS protections, auth, or security tests to make something pass.
- Changes to authN/authZ, crypto or secrets handling are High risk (AGENTS.md §3).
- Do not bypass Cursor/Claude permission or sandbox controls to finish a task.
- For deeper review, use the `security-and-hardening` skill.
